 problem, American Mathematical Monthly, vol. 83(7), pp. 521\u2013538, 1976.","journal-title":"American Mathematical Monthly"},{"issue":"1","key":"10_CR15","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","first-page":"64","DOI":"10.1198\/0003130042782","volume":"58","author":"R. Rodriguez","year":"2004","unstructured":"R. Rodriguez, First significant digit patterns from mixtures of uniform distributions, American Statistician, vol. 58(1), pp. 64\u201371, 2004.","journal-title":"American Statistician"},{"key":"10_CR16","unstructured":"P. Scott and M. Fasli, Benford\u2019s Law: An Empirical Investigation and a Novel Explanation, Technical Report CSM 349, Department of Computer Science, University of Essex, Colchester, United Kingdom, 2001."},{"key":"10_CR17","unstructured":"U.S. Census Bureau, Population Estimates \u2013 Counties, Washington, DC (www.census.gov\/popest\/counties), 1990."},{"issue":"3","key":"10_CR18","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","first-page":"219","DOI":"10.1007\/s11846-008-0019-9","volume":"2","author":"C. Watrin","year":"2008","unstructured":"C. Watrin, R. Struffert and R. Ullmann, Benford\u2019s law: An instrument for selecting tax audit targets? Review of Managerial Science, vol. 2(3), pp. 219\u2013237, 2008.","journal-title":"Review of Managerial Science"},{"key":"10_CR19","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","first-page":"200","DOI":"10.1007\/978-3-642-19376-7_18","volume-title":"Computational Forensics","author":"Y. Yannikos","year":"2011","unstructured":"Y. Yannikos, F. Franke, C. Winter and M. Schneider, 3LSPG: Forensic tool evaluation by three layer stochastic process based generation of data, in Computational Forensics, H. Sako, K. Franke and S. Saitoh (Eds.), Springer, Berlin, Germany, pp. 200\u2013211, 2011."}],"container-title":["IFIP Advances in Information and Communication Technology","Advances in Digital Forensics VII"],"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/link.springer.com\/content\/pdf\/10.1007\/978-3-642-24212-0_10.pdf","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"deposited":{"date-parts":[[2021,5,3]],"date-time":"2021-05-03T12:07:59Z","timestamp":1620043679000},"score":35.145634,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"http:\/\/link.springer.com\/10.1007\/978-3-642-24212-0_10"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2011]]},"ISBN":["9783642242113","9783642242120"],"references-count":19,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/978-3-642-24212-0_10","ISSN":["1868-4238","1861-2288"],"issn-type":[{"type":"print","value":"1868-4238"},{"type":"electronic","value":"1861-2288"}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2011]]}},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2025,3,25]],"date-time":"2025-03-25T14:46:42Z","timestamp":1742914002280,"version":"3.40.3"},"edition-number":"1","reference-count":0,"publisher":"Cambridge University Press","isbn-type":[{"type":"print","value":"9780521764704"},{"type":"print","value":"9780521748360"},{"type":"electronic","value":"9780511626807"}],"license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2010,2,3]],"date-time":"2010-02-03T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1265155200000},"content-version":"unspecified","delay-in-days":282,"URL":"https:\/\/www.cambridge.org\/core\/terms"}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2009,4,27]]},"DOI":"10.1017\/cbo9780511626807.002","type":"book-chapter","created":{"date-parts":[[2010,3,4]],"date-time":"2010-03-04T16:29:33Z","timestamp":1267720173000},"page":"12-29","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":1,"title":["A Forensics Approach to Detecting Election Fraud"],"prefix":"10.1017","member":"56","container-title":["The Forensics of Election Fraud"],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.cambridge.org\/core\/services\/aop-cambridge-core\/content\/view\/D3C0855FFE4A108D8260CE64B65B77FD","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"deposited":{"date-parts":[[2022,10,12]],"date-time":"2022-10-12T05:42:21Z","timestamp":1665553341000},"score":34.437317,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/www.cambridge.org\/core\/product\/identifier\/CBO9780511626807A010\/type\/book_part"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2009,4,27]]},"ISBN":["9780521764704","9780521748360","9780511626807"],"references-count":0,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1017\/cbo9780511626807.002","published":{"date-parts":[[2009,4,27]]}},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,22]],"date-time":"2026-07-22T08:02:08Z","timestamp":1784707328233,"version":"3.55.0"},"reference-count":48,"publisher":"SAGE Publications","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,19]],"date-time":"2026-07-19T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1784419200000},"content-version":"tdm","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"https:\/\/journals.sagepub.com\/page\/policies\/text-and-data-mining-license"}],"content-domain":{"domain":["journals.sagepub.com"],"crossmark-restriction":true},"short-container-title":["Statistical Modelling"],"abstract":"<jats:p>We introduce a novel extension of Benford\u2019s law that shifts attention from leading digits to the joint distribution of final digit pairs in numerical data. Traditional Benford-based tests rely on first-digit frequencies and require observations to span several orders of magnitude, a condition frequently violated in precinct-level election returns. To overcome this limitation, we develop a formal probabilistic framework for analyzing the behaviour of the last two digits under Benfordian assumptions, which we denote L2-BL 10. Within this framework, we derive closed-form expressions for digit-pair probabilities and establish convergence bounds toward their limiting uniform distribution, providing new theoretical results that extend the statistical foundations of Benford analysis beyond its classical first-digit form. We then show that precinct-level vote counts are well approximated by a discrete Weibull distribution, linking this theoretical development to realistic data-generating processes encountered in elections. Monte Carlo simulations and empirical illustrations using data from nine states in the 2016 and 2020 US presidential elections demonstrate how the L2-BL 10 framework can be operationalized as a practical diagnostic for identifying anomalous digit behaviour. The result is a method that is both theoretically grounded and directly applicable to election forensics in settings where standard Benford tests are not appropriate.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1177\/1471082x261457748","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,20]],"date-time":"2026-07-20T04:59:38Z","timestamp":1784523578000},"update-policy":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1177\/sage-journals-update-policy","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["A novel Benford-based approach for detecting statistical anomalies in precinct-level election data"],"prefix":"10.1177","author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-5000-7396","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Philip","family":"Eigen","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Institute for Statistical Policy Analysis, Advancing American Freedom, Washington, DC, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Steven J.","family":"Miller","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Mathematics and Statistics, Williams College, Williamstown, MA, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Kevin","family":"Dayaratna","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Institute for Statistical Policy Analysis, Advancing American Freedom, Washington, DC, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"179","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,19]]},"reference":[{"key":"e_1_3_4_2_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.3390\/stats5030049"},{"key":"e_1_3_4_3_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1016\/j.jnca.2013.09.007"},{"key":"e_1_3_4_4_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1093\/pan\/mps003"},{"key":"e_1_3_4_5_1","unstructured":"Benford F (1938) The law of anomalous numbers. Proceedings of the American Philosophical Society 78 551\u2013572.